<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/meeting.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/meeting-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/meeting.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="797" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/resources/">Resources</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal process</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker kicker-l">Process guide</p><h1>How Insurance Appraisal Works in Colorado</h1><p class="lede">Seven stages, from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where files most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>7</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div><svg class="ridge" viewBox="0 0 1440 90" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M0 90V62l110-30 60 22 130-50 90 44 60-16 120 38 150-62 80 34 90-20 130 44 110-34 70 18 140-48 100 40v48z"/></svg></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<p>Insurance appraisal is a contractual process written into most Colorado property policies. It exists to settle one kind of disagreement — the <em>amount</em> of a covered loss — without a lawsuit. Here is how a typical appraisal unfolds, stage by stage. Your policy’s wording controls, so read your own appraisal clause alongside this guide.</p>
<h2 id="stage-1">Stage 1 — The disagreement</h2>
<p>Appraisal begins with a claim the insurer has accepted, at least in part. The adjuster has inspected and issued an estimate; you or your contractor believe it’s too low. Before going further, put the disagreement in writing: what you think is missing, what your contractor’s number is and what documentation supports it. Many disputes end here when a supplement is approved.</p>
<h2 id="stage-2">Stage 2 — The written demand</h2>
<p>If the gap remains, either side may demand appraisal in writing. A demand typically identifies the claim, states that the parties disagree on the amount of loss, invokes the appraisal provision and names your appraiser or commits to naming one within the policy’s time frame. Send it in a way you can prove, and check whether your policy requires a sworn proof of loss or another step first.</p>
<h2 id="stage-3">Stage 3 — Both sides name appraisers</h2>
<p>Each party selects a competent and impartial appraiser — many clauses allow 20 days after the demand. Your appraiser reviews the file, inspects the property and builds a valuation; the insurer’s appraiser does the same. The two exchange estimates and begin working through the differences.</p>
<h2 id="stage-4">Stage 4 — Selecting an umpire</h2>
<p>The appraisers choose a neutral umpire, often early, so one is ready if needed. If they can’t agree within the time the policy allows (commonly 15 days), either party can ask a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. In Colorado that is typically the district court.</p>
<h2 id="stage-5">Stage 5 — Appraiser negotiation</h2>
<p>Most of the work happens here. The appraisers walk the property together, reconcile measurements, compare pricing and agree on as many line items as they can. A large share of appraisals end at this stage with both appraisers signing an agreed award, no umpire decision needed.</p>
<h2 id="stage-6">Stage 6 — Umpire review</h2>
<p>Items the appraisers can’t resolve go to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a position with photos, measurements and pricing support. The umpire may inspect, ask questions or hold an informal meeting, then decides each disputed item.</p>
<h2 id="stage-7">Stage 7 — The signed award</h2>
<p>An award signed by any two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss. A well-drafted award separates replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value, and may list items by coverage. The insurer then pays under the policy — less the deductible and prior payments — with recoverable depreciation typically paid once repairs are complete.</p>
<div class="callout"><p class="callout-t">Fees</p><p>Each side pays its own appraiser, and umpire fees are usually split equally. Ask any appraiser, including us, to put the fee arrangement in writing before you sign.</p></div>
<h2>What appraisal won’t do</h2>
<ul>
<li>It generally won’t decide whether the loss is covered.</li>
<li>It won’t raise your policy limits or remove your deductible.</li>
<li>It won’t award bad-faith damages, interest or attorney’s fees.</li>
<li>It won’t stop policy deadlines, including the time limit to sue.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Colorado notes</h2>
<p>The Colorado Division of Insurance, part of the Department of Regulatory Agencies, regulates insurers and handles consumer complaints. Colorado statutes also address an insurer’s unreasonable delay or denial of covered benefits (C.R.S. §§ 10-3-1115 and 10-3-1116). Those are legal claims outside appraisal; if you think they apply, talk to a Colorado attorney. Colorado law also prohibits roofing contractors from paying or waiving a homeowner’s insurance deductible — another reason to keep the claim math honest.</p>
<h2>Is your file ready?</h2>
<p>Before demanding appraisal, run through the <a href="/claim-planning-tools/appraisal-file-readiness-check/">appraisal file readiness check</a> and put your deadlines into the <a href="/claim-planning-tools/claim-timeline-builder/">claim timeline builder</a>.</p>
</article>
